WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.038
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Privileged Access.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:10.163
This is Chapter 5: PIM for
Groups and the P R V groups.

4
00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:14.534
A privileged role reaches a person
through one of these groups, so whoever

5
00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:19.337
controls the group controls the role, and
that control belongs in a reviewed file.

6
00:00:19.337 --> 00:00:23.982
We'll start from Microsoft's own request
samples, see what they leave out,

7
00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:29.099
then walk the file, the findings, the plan
and its guards, two labs and the dangers.

8
00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:34.620
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

9
00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.520
Why does this matter?

10
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.695
Because the program never hands a
privileged role to a person directly.

11
00:00:43.695 --> 00:00:46.695
It hands it to a group
with the tier in its name,

12
00:00:46.695 --> 00:00:49.695
so the tier shows up in
every review and alert.

13
00:00:49.695 --> 00:00:53.979
That means the group itself is now
the privilege: whoever adds a member,

14
00:00:53.979 --> 00:00:58.707
or changes the activation policy, has
effectively changed who holds the role.

15
00:00:58.707 --> 00:01:01.107
Chapter 1 already reads these groups.

16
00:01:01.107 --> 00:01:03.738
This chapter writes them,
from a reviewed file,

17
00:01:03.738 --> 00:01:07.060
and because people can still
change membership around it,

18
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:11.910
the code reads the tenant first and treats
anything it didn't put there as drift.

19
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.023
Next, which cloud.

20
00:01:17.023 --> 00:01:22.743
For a GCC tenant like this estate's, every
call goes to the worldwide Graph host,

21
00:01:22.743 --> 00:01:25.961
and sign-in goes to the
worldwide login page.

22
00:01:25.961 --> 00:01:29.498
GCC High and DoD have hosts of their own.

23
00:01:29.498 --> 00:01:34.670
Every PIM for Groups API page the chapter
read lists the US Government clouds too,

24
00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.681
and the governance
licensing PIM depends on is

25
00:01:37.681 --> 00:01:40.847
available in all three
government environments.

26
00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:46.320
The module never guesses: you name the
cloud on every run, and there's no default.

27
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:55.045
Two Microsoft features meet in a P R V
group, and it helps to keep them apart.

28
00:01:55.045 --> 00:01:59.493
A role-assignable group is the only kind
that can hold an Entra role; you choose

29
00:01:59.493 --> 00:02:03.733
that at creation and can never change
it, and there's a limit of five hundred.

30
00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:05.552
PIM for Groups is separate:

31
00:02:05.552 --> 00:02:09.770
just-in-time membership or
ownership, each with its own policy.

32
00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:13.683
The workbook's privileged-access
design combines them two ways.

33
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:18.233
For an Entra role, the group is eligible
and its members activate the role.

34
00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:21.620
For anything else, people are
eligible for the group itself.

35
00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.490
The file records which.

36
00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.265
Here's where a group's
privileged state actually lives.

37
00:02:30.265 --> 00:02:35.165
Not in the members list: eligible members
don't appear there until they activate.

38
00:02:35.165 --> 00:02:40.223
Instead there are separate collections for
eligible and active schedules, filtered

39
00:02:40.223 --> 00:02:44.640
by group, and two policies per group
whose IDs you only learn by reading them.

40
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.278
A few facts shape everything that follows.

41
00:02:47.278 --> 00:02:50.087
A group joins PIM the
first time you touch it,

42
00:02:50.087 --> 00:02:53.677
its policy IDs change when it
does, and it can never leave.

43
00:02:53.677 --> 00:02:56.225
And access packages are deferred to later,

44
00:02:56.225 --> 00:02:59.790
with one warning about expiry
dates carried forward.

45
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.415
Microsoft doesn't publish an end-to-end
tutorial for PIM for Groups in Graph.

46
00:03:08.415 --> 00:03:13.160
What it does publish is a handful of
single requests in the API reference,

47
00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.690
plus a how-to for updating policy rules.

48
00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.784
Those requests only exist inside web
pages, so the repository pins the page

49
00:03:20.784 --> 00:03:25.878
source unmodified, with hashes a test
checks and a weekly watch for changes.

50
00:03:25.878 --> 00:03:30.017
Then a small harness sends each
request, exactly as published,

51
00:03:30.017 --> 00:03:33.914
to a fake Graph that checks them
the way Learn says PIM does.

52
00:03:33.914 --> 00:03:37.050
Nothing signs in and
nothing leaves the machine.

53
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.562
This is the heart of the chapter.

54
00:03:42.562 --> 00:03:46.362
The request shapes are good, and
the plan copies them exactly.

55
00:03:46.362 --> 00:03:50.474
But run as published, the samples
go wrong in instructive ways.

56
00:03:50.474 --> 00:03:55.188
The first example is titled an ownership
request and actually grants membership.

57
00:03:55.188 --> 00:03:57.754
Its comment and its dates disagree,

58
00:03:57.754 --> 00:04:01.913
and the dates are from 2023, so
today the fake refuses them.

59
00:04:01.913 --> 00:04:05.975
Nothing reads the tenant first,
so a second run grants twice.

60
00:04:05.975 --> 00:04:10.025
And one sample is a user's
self-activation, not an admin's.

61
00:04:10.025 --> 00:04:15.060
So the verdict: keep the shapes, take
everything else from the reviewed file.

62
00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:19.748
Now the code.

63
00:04:19.748 --> 00:04:23.399
The chapter adds its own module,
its own list of allowed calls,

64
00:04:23.399 --> 00:04:28.073
and an entry script, and borrows nothing
from Chapter 1 except the cloud table.

65
00:04:28.073 --> 00:04:32.864
Everything is driven by one reviewed
file: for each group, who may be a

66
00:04:32.864 --> 00:04:38.084
member or owner, whether active or
eligible, until when, and on which ticket.

67
00:04:38.084 --> 00:04:43.157
A second file sets the activation policy
per tier, and it's labeled design guidance:

68
00:04:43.157 --> 00:04:48.229
shorter activation and approval for the
top tiers, longer eligibility lower down,

69
00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:52.080
and one-hour owner activation
with approval everywhere.

70
00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.705
Two details in the policy
deserve a closer look.

71
00:04:58.705 --> 00:05:02.533
Learn's pages disagree on how
long an activation may last:

72
00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:06.442
one says eight hours at most,
another says up to twenty-four.

73
00:05:06.442 --> 00:05:10.317
The code takes the stricter
reading and flags anything longer.

74
00:05:10.317 --> 00:05:13.480
Then there's the authentication
context from Chapter 3.

75
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:18.619
Every member and owner policy selects
it, as Book 04, Access and Privilege,

76
00:05:18.619 --> 00:05:23.672
requires, so the rules ask for
justification and a ticket instead of M F A.

77
00:05:23.672 --> 00:05:28.126
Learn says to turn the context's
policy on before PIM selects it,

78
00:05:28.126 --> 00:05:31.980
so the apply refuses unless
a person states that it's On.

79
00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.879
And it only forces a fresh sign-in on
every activation if its policy says so,

80
00:05:36.879 --> 00:05:39.530
and even then there's a ten-minute window.

81
00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:44.540
Learn also disagrees on whether it's preview,
so the chapter treats it as preview.

82
00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:49.991
Before anything is planned,

83
00:05:49.991 --> 00:05:53.540
the code reads the tenant and
checks it against nine rules.

84
00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:58.123
Some come straight from Learn: only
role-assignable groups can hold a role, PIM

85
00:05:58.123 --> 00:06:00.773
can't manage dynamic or
synchronized groups,

86
00:06:00.773 --> 00:06:03.065
and there's a hard limit of five hundred.

87
00:06:03.065 --> 00:06:08.168
Others are the program's design, and
they're labeled that way: no nesting at all,

88
00:06:08.168 --> 00:06:12.381
every entry has an end and a ticket,
no owners on tier zero groups,

89
00:06:12.381 --> 00:06:15.540
and anything the file
doesn't grant is a finding.

90
00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:19.152
One subtle one is the owner
trap, which we'll see in the lab.

91
00:06:19.152 --> 00:06:23.050
Activations are ignored, since
they aren't standing state.

92
00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.987
Once the differences are known, the plan
turns them into requests in a fixed order:

93
00:06:31.987 --> 00:06:35.935
create any missing groups, then
set the policy rules so they're

94
00:06:35.935 --> 00:06:40.038
in force before anyone is granted,
then grants, and removals last.

95
00:06:40.038 --> 00:06:44.362
Each request carries its permission
and a line of diff a reviewer can read.

96
00:06:44.362 --> 00:06:48.146
Nothing is sent unless you ask for
it, and a plan that selects the

97
00:06:48.146 --> 00:06:52.801
authentication context also needs a
person's statement that its policy is On.

98
00:06:52.801 --> 00:06:58.031
And even then, seven guards can refuse the
whole run: losing approval on a top tier,

99
00:06:58.031 --> 00:07:00.885
a permanent grant, a
group approving itself,

100
00:07:00.885 --> 00:07:06.630
or suddenly removing more people than
expected, which usually means a broken file.

101
00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:14.756
Learn isn't always consistent with
itself, and the chapter writes down

102
00:07:14.756 --> 00:07:18.830
every place it isn't, so a later edit
doesn't quietly loosen the code.

103
00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:20.843
Activation length is one.

104
00:07:20.843 --> 00:07:25.831
Whether PIM for Groups is generally
available or still beta is another:

105
00:07:25.831 --> 00:07:29.129
the API pages say one thing,
a how-to says another.

106
00:07:29.129 --> 00:07:32.991
The permissions for role-assignable
groups differ between pages,

107
00:07:32.991 --> 00:07:35.167
and the Lab 2 run is how you settle that.

108
00:07:35.167 --> 00:07:40.284
Even the obvious PowerShell cmdlet
disagrees with its own API page,

109
00:07:40.284 --> 00:07:43.200
so the code calls the API directly.

110
00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:48.012
Who is this for?

111
00:07:48.012 --> 00:07:53.127
Developers building the write path, the
identity engineers who own the groups

112
00:07:53.127 --> 00:07:57.376
and the file, and the I S S O's who
review each plan before it's applied.

113
00:07:57.376 --> 00:07:59.187
Then, what the code may do.

114
00:07:59.187 --> 00:08:01.887
Every call gets its
least-privileged permission.

115
00:08:01.887 --> 00:08:05.301
The read side needs four permissions
and none of them can write.

116
00:08:05.301 --> 00:08:09.112
The plan lists exactly the
permissions its own changes need,

117
00:08:09.112 --> 00:08:12.162
so the app that applies it
holds those and no more.

118
00:08:12.162 --> 00:08:16.929
And the one powerful permission, the
one that can assign any directory role,

119
00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:21.210
is kept off the pipeline entirely:
create these groups by hand.

120
00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.860
Lab 1 runs on a laptop: PowerShell 7
and Pester, no tenant and no network.

121
00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:34.023
First you send Microsoft's
samples, as published,

122
00:08:34.023 --> 00:08:37.660
to the fake, and the tests
confirm each gap from the table.

123
00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:41.691
Then you send the first example
by hand and see it for yourself:

124
00:08:41.691 --> 00:08:44.616
it's titled ownership,
it asks for membership,

125
00:08:44.616 --> 00:08:48.410
and the fake refuses it because
its end date passed in 2023.

126
00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:52.335
Next you list the read permissions,
four and none that can write.

127
00:08:52.335 --> 00:08:56.135
Finally you plan against a
fictional tenant, as a dry run.

128
00:08:56.135 --> 00:08:57.850
Let's read what it found.

129
00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.875
Reading the results is where
the rules become concrete.

130
00:09:04.875 --> 00:09:09.925
On the tier zero group, someone holds
permanent membership the file never granted,

131
00:09:09.925 --> 00:09:12.673
and an owner is eligible
with no active owner,

132
00:09:12.673 --> 00:09:16.238
which is exactly the trap where
activation can't be undone.

133
00:09:16.238 --> 00:09:19.781
The policy also allows
activation without approval,

134
00:09:19.781 --> 00:09:24.613
for twice as long as it should, and with
M F A instead of the activation gate.

135
00:09:24.613 --> 00:09:27.812
On the Azure group, another
group is nested inside.

136
00:09:27.812 --> 00:09:31.637
The diff shows each change
in a line, removals last.

137
00:09:31.637 --> 00:09:35.325
Then set a tight removal limit
and watch a guard refuse to run.

138
00:09:35.325 --> 00:09:38.961
Finally apply to the fake:
it refuses until you state

139
00:09:38.961 --> 00:09:42.175
the gate's policy is On,
then sends ten requests.

140
00:09:42.175 --> 00:09:44.040
And run the full suite.

141
00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:51.971
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant with
fictional people, never production,

142
00:09:51.971 --> 00:09:56.302
and because a group can never leave
PIM, you make groups just for the lab.

143
00:09:56.302 --> 00:09:59.044
Create them by hand, register two apps,

144
00:09:59.044 --> 00:10:02.140
a reader and a writer,
and plan as the reader.

145
00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.980
Then confirm the activation
gate's policy is On,

146
00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:08.403
apply as the writer, and compare
the portal with your file.

147
00:10:08.403 --> 00:10:13.321
This lab also settles open questions:
whether the narrower permissions are enough,

148
00:10:13.321 --> 00:10:15.815
and whether the policy update really took.

149
00:10:15.815 --> 00:10:18.553
Rerun to see zero
changes, try one removal,

150
00:10:18.553 --> 00:10:23.820
and then back everything out, because
the output names privileged people.

151
00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:30.632
Each of these dangers has
a matching rule or guard.

152
00:10:30.632 --> 00:10:34.858
The quietest is drift: someone
changes membership in the portal,

153
00:10:34.858 --> 00:10:40.120
and nobody ever reviews it, so every run
reads first and flags what it didn't grant.

154
00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:43.245
The sharpest is dropping
approval on a top-tier group,

155
00:10:43.245 --> 00:10:47.932
which Learn specifically warns lets a
lesser admin act on someone else's behalf.

156
00:10:47.932 --> 00:10:51.908
And the bluntest is a truncated
file that would remove everyone,

157
00:10:51.908 --> 00:10:55.958
which is why removals go last and
a guard stops more than a handful.

158
00:10:55.958 --> 00:11:00.050
Notice again: each guard is
code that runs every time.

159
00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.150
Here's what done looks like.

160
00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:08.374
On the group side: a file
that passes its own check,

161
00:11:08.374 --> 00:11:10.873
with an end and a ticket on every entry;

162
00:11:10.873 --> 00:11:14.742
role-assignable groups wherever
a role is held; no nesting,

163
00:11:14.742 --> 00:11:19.579
and no owners on tier zero; and policies
that match the reviewed settings,

164
00:11:19.579 --> 00:11:23.287
with the activation gate
selected once its policy is On.

165
00:11:23.287 --> 00:11:26.722
On the run side: approver groups
with at least two people,

166
00:11:26.722 --> 00:11:31.649
a plan reviewed by someone other than its
author, a rerun that shows nothing left

167
00:11:31.649 --> 00:11:36.501
to change, apps holding exactly what they
need, and pinned pages still matching.

168
00:11:36.501 --> 00:11:40.337
Next, Chapter 6 takes
PIM to Azure resources.

169
00:11:40.337 --> 00:11:41.500
Thanks for watching.
